# I03.var08 bounded CLI signoff

Decision: **GO_BOUNDED_SEALED_ORIGINAL_NATIVE**, contingent on the separate evidence-content and Git publication gates. The accepted C08 exact-limit run is read-only context and was not repeated. One new run, `i03-var08-idle-maxut-out-v100-ssh-20261008-01`, completed naturally under the reviewed zero-native preflight and fresh launch approval. Root's native receipt `tmp/i03-var08-root-native-dispatch-20261008-01/receipt.json` records the actual transport exit, original streams, guards and 39 sealed native-time originals. The independent [postrun memo](review/independent-postrun-go-2026-10-08.md) binds its separate sealed replay and audit.

The source changes only PDESC and adds the h24.75 P-CREW OUT row. The request reduces nominal quantity from two to one; the observed positive task start removes one physical local unit after the h24.5 preparation releases and printed MAXUT remaining zero. Arrival confirmation does not remove another unit. Terminal local and nominal quantities are one, reservation, pending OUT and backorder are zero, and two aircraft are ready. The h28–29 mission is unchanged. Author validation checked 113 timed records and 5,210 child report cells. Independent validation checked 5,210 parent and 5,210 child cells and all 55 predeclared differences. Report presentation constants and binary32 means were separately specified before launch.

The converter's exact summary is Errors 0, Warnings 2, Notes 0; the only warnings are the predeclared Control #108 and System #126 messages. Original stream, source-map, invocation, closure and checker receipt pins are retained. The current evidence index and postrun reproduction guide govern read-only replay after catalog append.

This signoff is limited to the fixed source, installed executable and original run. It does not identify a person outside the local/task ledger or establish a general MAXUT eligibility law, transfer timing law, GUI behavior, performance, cross-engine equivalence or full I03 coverage. The earlier prospective README remains a historical expectation, and Git publication remains a separate decision.
